<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Managed App Configuration — Princeton production display

  The four production channels, locked down: plays on launch, recovers from network
  drops, preset list is the only affordance, no on-device settings at all.

  Copy it in with `./scripts/mdm-payload.sh production` — Jamf takes the <dict> alone
  and rejects the wrapper, so do not paste this file directly.

  WHAT THE TWO LOCKS DO, because they are not interchangeable:

    ViewOnlyMode      Reduces the main screen to the preset list. No URL entry, no
                      Play/Clear, no Manage Stream Presets. Pressing a preset plays it
                      immediately. On its own it deliberately LEAVES Settings reachable,
                      so the mode can be switched off from the device.

    SettingsDisabled  Disables the Settings link outright.

  Setting both, as here, means there is no on-device recourse of any kind. That is the
  intent for an unattended display, with one consequence worth stating plainly:

  SettingsPIN IS INERT IN THIS COMBINATION. The PIN prompt lives inside the Settings
  screen, and SettingsDisabled stops that screen being reachable, so the PIN can never be
  entered. It is omitted here rather than set to a value that does nothing. If you want a
  technician to be able to get in on site, drop SettingsDisabled and set SettingsPIN
  instead — managed keys still cannot be edited, and a reconcile overwrites anything else
  they change.

  BEFORE APPLYING THIS, verify that a payload change reconciles while the app runs — test
  2 in docs/HARDWARE-VERIFICATION.md, using jamf-app-config-verify.xml, which leaves the
  UI open. Reconcile is the escape hatch this payload depends on, and as of 1.1 it has
  never been observed against a real MDM write. If it turns out not to work, recovering a
  display locked by this payload means a force-quit or unscoping the app.

  EVERY CHANNEL HERE EXPIRES AFTER 24 HOURS OF CONTINUOUS PLAYBACK. All of them are
  Kaltura-backed, and playManifest returns a master playlist whose variant URLs are
  CloudFront-signed with a DateLessThan 24 hours out (verified 2026-08-14). An HLS client
  refetches the *media* playlist continuously but the *master* only once, so once the
  signature lapses those refetches start failing and the stream stalls where it is.

  Recovering means building a new player item, which refetches the master and gets fresh
  signatures — which is exactly what AutoResume's stall detection does, and exactly what
  has never been observed working (issue #9).

  The useful consequence: this is a scheduled, deterministic reproduction of the failure
  that has been impossible to trigger on demand. Leave a display on one channel for over
  24 hours. If it is still playing afterwards, recovery works. If it is frozen, #9 is
  confirmed on hardware and this payload should not be deployed unattended until it is
  fixed — a locked display with no on-device recourse would need a daily visit or a
  Jamf-scheduled restart.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Name the actual display. Shown as the screen heading. -->
  <key>DisplayTitle</key>
  <string>Sherrerd Hall</string>

  <key>PlayOnAppOpen</key>
  <true/>

  <key>AutoResume</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Preset list is the only control. -->
  <key>ViewOnlyMode</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- No Settings screen. See the note above about SettingsPIN. -->
  <key>SettingsDisabled</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- One of the six values the UI offers, unlike the deliberately odd 7 in the verify
       payload. Also sets the stall threshold, which is max(15, RetryTimeout * 3) — so 30s
       here before a stalled stream is treated as needing a reload. -->
  <key>RetryTimeout</key>
  <real>10.0</real>

  <key>ChannelPresets</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>News</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/news</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>News Plus</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/news-plus</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Scenic</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/scenic</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Announcements</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/announcements</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Graduate</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/graduate</string>
    </dict>
    <!-- Undergraduate and Live Events are direct Kaltura entry URLs rather than
         orfe.princeton.edu redirects. Worth knowing operationally: a redirect can be
         repointed server-side, but an entry id baked into this payload needs a Jamf edit
         and a reconcile on every display when the entry is replaced. -->
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Undergraduate</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://cdnapi.kaltura.com/p/1449362/sp/144936200/playManifest/entryId/1_aewpakod/format/applehttp/protocol/https/a.m3u8</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Name</key>
      <string>Live Events</string>
      <key>URL</key>
      <string>https://cdnapi.kaltura.com/p/1449362/sp/144936200/playManifest/entryId/1_kt4nchs4/format/applehttp/protocol/https/a.m3u8</string>
    </dict>
  </array>

  <!-- Must match one of the URLs above, or the display starts on nothing. Change this to
       pick which channel a given display comes up on. -->
  <key>DefaultChannel</key>
  <string>https://orfe.princeton.edu/live/news</string>
</dict>
</plist>
